QB.add(54,[{"n":1,"q":"A 12 y/o girl presents with headaches, vomiting, and altered mental status. MRI shows obstructive hydrocephalus caused by multiple cysts blocking cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Viral encephalitis"},{"t":"Neurocysticercosis"},{"t":"Bacterial meningitis"},{"t":"Multiple sclerosi"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The obstructive hydrocephalus seen on MRI is consistent with cysticercal cysts blocking CSF flow, a known complication of neurocysticercosis. This condition can cause increased intracranial pressure, leading to headaches, vomiting, and altered mental status."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurocysticercosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features Stages of lesion"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Seizures: The most common symptom , typically Vesicular: Cystic lesions with scolex. The parasite is new-onset partial seizures that may generalize. viable. ( Live stage) Headaches Colloidal: Cysts degenerate, causing surrounding Hydrocephalus: Caused by cysts obstructing oedema. (Degenerating stage) cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow, leading to increased Granulonodular: Cysts with less oedema, more pressure. solidified. (Degenerating stage) Cognitive and Personality Changes Nodular-Calcified: Parasite is no longer viable. Cysts Focal Neurological Deficits (weakness or sensory calcify, with no active inflammation. (Dead stage) disturbances) Increased Intracranial Pressure (nausea, vomiting, ataxia, visual deficits, papilledema)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Viral encephalitis (Option A): Viral encephalitis can cause altered mental status but is typically associated with diffuse brain inflammation rather than cysts causing hydrocephalus. The presence of cysts on MRI suggests neurocysticercosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bacterial meningitis (Option C): Bacterial meningitis causes diffuse meningeal inflammation and purulent CSF rather than cyst formation and hydrocephalus. The cysts and hydrocephalus seen on imaging are more indicative of neurocysticercosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis (Option D): Multiple sclerosis involves demyelination rather than cyst formation and does not typically cause hydrocephalus. The patient’s clinical presentation and imaging findings suggest neurocysticercosis."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 45 y/o man presents with fever, a headache, and focal neurological deficits on the right side. He had dental surgery two weeks ago. CT shows a ring-enhancing lesion with surrounding oedema in the left temporal lobe. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Low-grade gliomaB)"},{"t":"Primary brain tumour"},{"t":"Metastatic brain tumour"},{"t":"Brain abscess"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brain abscesses result from infections that spread from nearby or distant sites. This patient’s recent dental surgery is a potential source of infection, which, along with his fever, headache, and focal neurological deficits, strongly points towards a brain abscess. The presence of a ring-enhancing lesion on CT, which is characteristic of an abscess, further supports this diagnosis."},{"k":"img","v":8112},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ring-enhancing lesion on CT - Brain Abscess"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Brain Tumor and Brain Abscess"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Brain Tumour Brain Abscess"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cause Abnormal growth of cells in the Localised collection of pus due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"brain or meninges. infection."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms Headaches, nausea, and vomiting Headaches, fever (in about half of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"due to raised intracranial pressure. cases), focal deficits, and Seizures and gradual neurological sometimes seizures. deficits."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis MRI and CT scan MRI is preferred, CT-guided"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Biopsy confirms the type and grade aspiration identifies the infectious"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"agent."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Surgery, radiotherapy, High-dose intravenous antibiotics"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chemotherapy. and surgical drainage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Low-grade glioma (Option A): Low-grade gliomas tend to grow slowly and present with a more gradual onset of symptoms. Fever and acute focal neurological deficits are not typical for low-grade gliomas. Low grade glioma won't be ring enhancing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary brain tumour (Option B): While primary brain tumours can also cause headaches and neurological deficits, they do not typically present with fever. They are also less commonly associated with preceding infections or surgeries. Primary brain tumor won't be ring enhancing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Metastatic brain tumour (Option C): Although metastatic tumours can appear as ring-enhancing lesions on imaging, the patient has no known history of cancer, and metastatic brain tumours are not associated with fever or recent infections, making this diagnosis less probable."}]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following is the most common brain tumour in childhood?","o":[{"t":"Pilocytic astrocytoma"},{"t":"Medulloblastoma"},{"t":"Ependymoma"},{"t":"Metastasis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common brain tumour found in children. It is a slow-growing, low-grade (Grade 1) tumour that typically occurs in the cerebellum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Under the age of 2- Common tumours are choroid plexus papillomas, anaplastic astrocytomas, and teratomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the first decade- Common tumors include medulloblastomas, astrocytomas, ependymomas, craniopharyngiomas, and gliomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metastases are rare in children but neuroblastoma metastases are the most frequent when they occur. The most common brain tumour in adults is Metastasis. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Although rare, brain tumours are the most common type of childhood cancer after leukaemia and lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most tumors in children are located infratentorially ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microscopic Features : Long, thin bipolar processes (pilocytic)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key Pathological Findings : Rosenthal fibers, eosinophilic granular bodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cellularity : Low-to-moderate, multinucleated giant cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Features : Hemosiderin-laden macrophages, calcifications (rare necrosis)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infiltration : May infiltrate surrounding brain tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis : Challenging to differentiate from low-grade diffuse astrocytoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immunohistochemistry : Positive for GFAP, S100, OLIG2; negative for neurofilament, chromogranin, CD34."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology of Pilocytic astrocytoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pilocytic astrocytoma (Grade 1): This slow-growing tumour is the most common brain tumour found in children."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common childhood malignant brain tumors: (Option B & C ruled out) Medulloblastomas High-grade gliomas Ependymomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medulloblastomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-grade gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ependymomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medulloblastomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-grade gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ependymomas."}]},{"n":4,"q":"Match the following syndromes with their most commonly seen brain tumours respectively. Von Hippel Landau a) Bilateral vestibular schwannomas Tuberous sclerosis b) Glioma Neurofibromatosis 2 c) Hemangioblastoma of the cerebellum Li Fraumeni Syndrome d) Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma\nVon Hippel Landau a) Bilateral vestibular schwannomas\nTuberous sclerosis b) Glioma\nNeurofibromatosis 2 c) Hemangioblastoma of the cerebellum\nLi Fraumeni Syndrome d) Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","o":[{"t":"1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b"},{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a"},{"t":"1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c"},{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Syndrome Associated Tumours"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cowden's syndrome Dysplastic cerebellar gangliocytoma (Lhermitte-Duclos"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"disease), meningioma, astrocytoma, breast, endometrial, thyroid cancer, trichilemmomas"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gardner's syndrome Medulloblastoma, glioblastoma, craniopharyngioma,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"familial polyposis, multiple osteomas, skin and soft tissue tumours"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome Gliomas, medulloblastomas, sarcomas, breast cancer,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"leukaemia, and others"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lynch syndrome Glioblastomas and other gliomas"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 Pituitary adenoma, malignant schwannoma and (Wermer's syndrome) pancreatic islet cell tumours"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"NF1 Schwannomas, astrocytomas, optic nerve gliomas,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"meningiomas, neurofibromas, neurofibrosarcomas, and others"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"NF2 Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, astrocytomas,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"multiple meningiomas, ependymomas"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tuberous Sclerosis (Bourneville Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, ependymomas, disease) glioma, ganglioneuroma, hamartoma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Turcot syndrome Gliomas, medulloblastomas"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Von Hippel Landau Hemangioblastomas of the cerebellum, retinal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"angiomas, renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, pancreatic tumours and cysts, endolymphatic sac tumours of the middle ear"}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 40 y/o man with a known brain tumour has recently undergone radiation therapy. During a follow-up visit, his doctor ordered imaging studies to evaluate the tumour. The physician wants to determine whether the changes observed on the imaging are due to tumour progression or necrosis resulting from treatment. Which imaging technique can help differentiate between the two?","o":[{"t":"Computed Tomography (CT)"},{"t":"Gadolinium-enhanced Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)"},{"t":"MR Spectroscopy"},{"t":"Functional MRI"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MR Spectroscopy provides insights into the chemical composition of the tissue, helping to distinguish between tumour progression and necrosis due to treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Modalities used in Brain tumours:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MR perfusion and spectroscopy provide insights into the blood flow and tissue composition, helping to distinguish tumour progression from treatment-related tissue necrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Computed Tomography (CT scan): CT scans can detect calcium buildup, haemorrhage, and hydrocephalus. They are quick and often used in emergencies but are no longer the standard for brain tumour evaluation. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI especially Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is the gold standard for diagnosing brain and spinal tumors . It offers higher resolution, better anatomical detail, and information about blood flow and tumour cell density. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functional MRI (fMRI) is valuable in presurgical planning as it helps define eloquent sensory, motor, and language cortices by assessing the distance between brain functions and tumours, facilitating surgery in critical areas. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Positron Emission Tomography (PET) measures the metabolic activity of brain lesions by tracking glucose use , showing malignant tissue as brighter due to its higher glucose consumption compared to normal tissue."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 22 y/o man is suspected to have a brain tumour after presenting with several concerning symptoms. He also reports episodes of nausea and difficulty with balance. Which of the following features when present will you not suspect brain tumors?","o":[{"t":"Persistent headaches"},{"t":"Seizures"},{"t":"Behaviour changes"},{"t":"Decreased skin sensitivity to temperature"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased skin sensitivity to temperature is more commonly associated with spinal tumours , as they can affect the spinal cord and disrupt sensory pathways."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Brain Tumors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In infants , a rapidly widening head or bulging crown is the most obvious sign of a brain tumour."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common symptoms in pediatric brain tumours include headaches, seizures, feelings of pressure inside the skull, nausea, and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sudden onset of vision problems can also indicate a brain tumour in children."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headaches are the most common symptom in older children and adults, often worsening over time and becoming more frequent. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures that start in adulthood with no underlying cause are a key warning sign of a brain tumour. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, vision or hearing problems, and personality or behaviour changes. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive changes can include problems with speech, language, thinking, and memory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor problems may manifest as weakness, lack of coordination, or loss of sensation in limbs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balance issues can include dizziness, trouble walking, and loss of equilibrium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydrocephalus and increased intracranial pressure can occur when a tumour blocks cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additional symptoms may include endocrine disorders, difficulty swallowing, facial paralysis, fatigue, weakened sense of smell, and changes in sleep patterns."}]},{"n":7,"q":"Identify the correct statements on CNS tumours. Calcifications are found in Meningioma and Oligodendroglioma. Rathke’s cyst and Craniopharyngioma are present as suprasellar masses. Meningioma and Glioblastoma multiforme never cross the midline. Giant cell Astrocytoma and Ependymoma occur as Intraventricular tumours. Schwannoma is an important Cerebello-pontine angle tumour. Metastatic lesions are never present in the suprasellar area.","o":[{"t":"2,3,5,6"},{"t":"1,2,4,5"},{"t":"1,2,5,6"},{"t":"All the above"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Important Facts on CNS Tumours"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sellar/ Suprasellar Tumours Cerebello-Pontine Angle Tumors ( Statement 2 & Statement 6 ) (Statement 5)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pituitary Adenoma Schwannoma Craniopharyngioma Meningioma Rathke’s cyst Epidermoid Meningioma (Presence of Dural tail) Arachnoid cyst Schwannoma Paraganglioma Dermoid Metastasis Germinoma Metastasis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Calcifications in CNS Tumours Midline Crossing CNS Tumours (Statement 1) (Statement 3)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Meningioma Glioblastoma multiforme Craniopharyngioma Radiation necrosis Oligodendroglioma Meningioma Chordoma Lymphoma Ependymoma Epidermoid cysts Metastasis Multiple Sclerosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tumour mimics Intraventricular Tumours"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Statement 4)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Abscess Ependymoma Multiple sclerosis (Tumefactive) Subependymoma Aneurysm Meningioma Infections- Eg. Herpes encephalitis, tuberculoma, Choroid Plexus Papilloma neurocysticercosis Giant cell Astrocytoma Dural AV Fistula"}]},{"n":8,"q":"Which of the following is the most common primary malignant brain tumour in adults?","o":[{"t":"Meningioma"},{"t":"Glioma"},{"t":"Vestibular schwannoma"},{"t":"CNS Lymphoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gliomas are the most common primary malignant brain tumours, with glioblastoma multiforme being the most aggressive subtype (Grade 4)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common brain tumour: Metastasis Small cell cancer of lung/ oat cell cancer Breast cancer Malignant melanoma Prostate Cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small cell cancer of lung/ oat cell cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Breast cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignant melanoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prostate Cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common primary malignant tumour: Glioma-Glioblastoma Multiforme (Grade 4)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common primary benign cancer is meningioma (Intracranial calcifications). Meningioma (Option A ruled out) Vestibular schwannoma (Option C ruled out) CNS Lymphoma (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meningioma (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vestibular schwannoma (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS Lymphoma (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overall Metastasis are three times more common than all primary brain tumours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incidence wise: METS > Meningioma > Glioma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common Brain tumour in Childhood is pilocytic astrocytoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small cell cancer of lung/ oat cell cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Breast cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignant melanoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prostate Cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meningioma (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vestibular schwannoma (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS Lymphoma (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk Factors for CNS Tumors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meningiomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Schwannomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary CNS lymphoma"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 32 y/o female presents to the ED with seizures and headaches. An MRI Contrast of the brain is given below. The neurologist is considering treatment options based on the imaging findings. Given the presence of multiple viable cystic lesions on the CT scan, which of the following treatment regimens is most appropriate?","o":[{"t":"Albendazole only"},{"t":"Praziquantel only"},{"t":"Albendazole + Praziquantel"},{"t":"Prednisolone only"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurocysticercosis is an infection caused by the larval stage of Taenia solium , leading to the formation of viable cystic lesions in the brain. Symptoms typically include seizures and headaches, with diagnosis supported by imaging findings of cysts particularly the identification of cystic lesions with scolex on MRI Contrast scan given."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The combination of albendazole and praziquantel is recommended for effective treatment of multiple viable cysts (>2 cysts)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Neurocysticercosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Albendazole : Drug of choice for parenchymal neurocysticercosis (15 mg/kg daily for 10-14 days). Recommended for 1-2 parenchymal cysts. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Praziquantel : Alternative option (50 mg/kg in 3 divided doses daily for 10 days). (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Albendazole + Praziquantel : For >2 viable cysts (Praziquantel: 50 mg/kg/day for 10-14 days)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prednisolone: 10 mg three times daily for 14 days, starting 1 day before albendazole or praziquantel. Given to suppress the inflammation during the natural course of the disease. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Viable Cysts : Treated with anticysticidal therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vesicular & Colloidal Stages : Treated with anticysticidal therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Granulonodular Stage : Some experts recommend treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcified Lesions : Do not require treatment with anticysticidal therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small, Solitary Cerebral Cysts : Often resolve without treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5. Operative Intervention : Indicated for hydrocephalus."}],"qi":[8086]},{"n":10,"q":"A 30 y/o male presents to the clinic with progressive headaches, fever, and weight loss over the past month. Neurological examination reveals focal deficits and a CT scan of the brain is given below. The patient also exhibits signs of meningitis. Based on the clinical presentation and CT findings, which of the following conditions is most likely diagnosed?","o":[{"t":"Neurocysticercosis"},{"t":"Tuberculoma"},{"t":"Brain abscess"},{"t":"Metastasis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms of progressive headaches, fever, weight loss , focal neurological deficits, and the presence of larger, conglomerated lesions on the CT scan suggest tuberculoma, which is commonly associated with meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Feature Neurocysticercosis Tuberculoma of the Brain"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lesions Smaller (< 20 mm), single or Larger (> 20 mm), often multiple,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"multiple conglomerated. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Associated Meningitis Absent Present"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Location Junction of grey-white matter Common in the posterior fossa"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MR Spectroscopy Multiple amino acid peaks Lipid peaks"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Neurological Absent Present Deficits"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Raised ICP Transient Present"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Constitutional Symptoms Absent Present"},{"k":"img","v":8086},{"k":"img","v":8092},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT images"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated) ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brain Abscess (Option C): A brain abscess usually presents as a singular lesion and may not show the conglomerated appearance described. While it can cause focal neurological deficits and systemic symptoms, it is less likely to present with the combination of signs of meningitisand multiple large lesions."},{"k":"img","v":8126},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT Brain showing Cerebral abscess and aspirations from the abscess."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Metastasis (Option D): Metastatic lesions can be multiple, and larger but they are usually ill-defined and typically do not present with significant meningitis. Additionally, the combination of fever, weight loss, and neurological deficits is more indicative of tuberculoma, especially given the patient's history and presentation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT Brain showing Metastasis of breast cancer."}],"qi":[8092]},{"n":11,"q":"A 28 y/o female presents to the neurology clinic with recurrent headaches and occasional seizures. An MRI of the brain is performed to evaluate her symptoms. The gadolinium-enhanced MRI shows cystic lesions as shown in the image below. Based on the MRI findings described, which stage of the clinical condition does this lesion represent?","o":[{"t":"Vesicular Stage"},{"t":"Colloidal Stage"},{"t":"Granulonodular Stage"},{"t":"Calcified Stage"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurocysticercosis is a parasitic infection of the central nervous system caused by the larvae of Taenia solium , presenting with seizures, headaches, and neurological deficits. Diagnosis is typically confirmed through imaging (CT or MRI) showing cystic lesions, often with visible scolex, and supported by serological tests."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The vesicular Stage is characterized by cystic lesions containing a visible scolex, often described as having a \"hole-with-dot\" appearance on MRI . There is no significant surrounding oedema or abnormal enhancement on gadolinium-enhanced MRI, which aligns with the findings described in the clinical scenario."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MRI (Gadolinium-Enhanced) Features in Neurocysticercosis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is crucial for identifying and differentiating between the stages of neurocysticercosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cystic lesions with a visible scolex ( \"hole-with-dot\" appearance )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No significant surrounding oedema or abnormal enhancement on gadolinium-enhanced MRI."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions show peripheral ring enhancement after gadolinium contrast administration. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Substantial surrounding oedema is visible, particularly on T2-weighted images."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions exhibit homogeneous enhancement on post-contrast MRI. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No surrounding oedema is present, as seen on FLAIR images."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No significant enhancement on gadolinium MRI. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcifications are better seen with susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI), as they may not enhance well on standard MRI."},{"k":"img","v":8132},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A: MRI of a vesicular cyst- Well-defined scolex, minimal contrast enhancement, and mass effect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B: MRI of a colloidal cyst- Ring enhancement, loss of the scolex, and perilesional oedema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"C: MRI of the nodular/granular stage- Nodule with diffuse enhancement and no cystic component."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"D: Noncontrast CT showing multiple punctuate calcifications."}],"qi":[8099]},{"n":12,"q":"A 35 y/o male presents with new-onset seizures and persistent headaches. He gives a history of travel to China a month ago. A CT scan of the brain is performed, revealing several ill-defined lesions with surrounding oedema and ring enhancement. Based on these imaging findings, which of the following is the confirmatory test for the condition?","o":[{"t":"Gadolinium-enhanced MRI"},{"t":"Western blot"},{"t":"Susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI)"},{"t":"Fundoscopic Exam"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of new-onset seizures and persistent headaches, combined with a history of travel to China (an endemic region for neurocysticercosis), raises suspicion of a parasitic infection affecting the CNS. The CT findings of several ill-defined lesions with surrounding oedema and ring enhancement are characteristic of stages commonly associatedwith neurocysticercosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In this context, the Western Blot serves as the confirmatory test by detecting antibodies to Taenia solium antigens."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigation Modalities in Neurocysticercosis :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Computed Tomography (CT):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vesicular Stage: Cystic lesions are well-defined, often containing the scolex . ( Live stage)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Colloidal: Ring enhancement, loss of the scolex, and perilesional oedema. (Degenerating stage)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Granulonodular Stages : Nodule with diffuse enhancement and no cystic component. (Degenerating stage)"},{"k":"img","v":8138},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcified Stage: Small hyperdense nodules (calcifications) are easily identified on CT without surrounding oedema or contrast enhancement. (Dead stage)"},{"k":"img","v":8143},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subarachnoid Neurocysticercosis: Hydrocephalus due to occlusion of foramina (Luschka and Magendie) may be visible. Basal arachnoiditis may show leptomeningeal enhancement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Enzyme-Linked Immunotransfer Blot (ELISA):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detects antibodies to Taenia solium antigens. It is highly specific but has reduced sensitivity in cases with minimal disease or only calcifications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antigen detection tests using monoclonal antibodies can detect viable infections, especially useful in subarachnoid neurocysticercosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Wester Blot: Useful for exposure and diagnosis but may yield false negatives in patients with minimal disease. Recommended for confirmatory diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Susceptibility-Weighted Imaging (SWI): MRI technique that is particularly useful for detecting small calcified lesions in the brain. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5. Fundoscopic Exam: Recommended for all patients with suspected neurocysticercosis to check for subretinal parasites . (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"6. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is crucial for identifying and differentiating between the stages of neurocysticercosis. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ventricular Cysts: Often visualized on MRI due to their differing signal properties from cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Ventricular cyst migration can also be detected."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"7. Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful in identifying segmental narrowing or occlusion of intracranial arteries in subarachnoid neurocysticercosis cases."}],"qi":[8086]},{"n":13,"q":"A 52 y/o man with a history of hypertension suddenly collapses. Imaging confirms a large left-sided brain infarction. His ICP is elevated, and the neurosurgical team is concerned about herniation. What type of herniation is most commonly associated with increased ICP?","o":[{"t":"Subfalcine herniation"},{"t":"Foraminal herniation"},{"t":"Transtentorial herniation"},{"t":"Cingulate herniation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A transtentorial herniation is the most common type of brain herniatio n and involves the medial temporal gyrus (uncus) being pushed through the tentorium cerebelli due to increased ICP. It can lead to the following clinical features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Compression of the oculomotor nerve leading to ipsilateral pupillary dilation ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kernohan-Woltman sign : In some cases, the displaced brain tissue compresses the opposite cerebral peduncle against the tentorium, resulting in a Babinski sign and ipsilateral hemiparesis , a condition referred to as the Kernohan-Woltman sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain infarctions: The anterior and posterior cerebral arteries can be compressed as they pass over the tentorium, potentially causing brain infarctions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydrocephalus : The herniation can block cerebrospinal fluid pathways, leading to ventricular entrapment and hydrocephalus ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coma"},{"k":"img","v":8150},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Different types of brain herniations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal ICP: In adults, normal intracranial pressure (ICP) ranges from 10-15 mmHg .The upper limit of normal ICP is 15 mmHg, with 20 mmHg used as an upper acceptable limit in intensive care. ICP>20-25mmHg for >5min requires immediate intervention."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain Infarction (Ischaemic Stroke): Caused by reduced blood flow to part of the brain, leading to tissue damage. Increases ICP , worsening brain function through compression and reduced cerebral perfusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain Haemorrhage : Occurs when a blood vessel in the brain ruptures, causing bleeding into brain tissue or surrounding areas. Raises ICP , causing brainstem compression and reduced blood flow."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subfalcine herniation (Option A): This occurs when the cingulate gyrus is pushed under the falx cerebri., crossing the midline. It is common but often asymptomatic or has mild symptoms, and is not typically the most dangerous form of herniation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Foraminal herniation (Option B): In foraminal/tonsillar herniation, the cerebellar tonsils are forced downward through the foramen magnum, which can compress the brainstem and disrupt vital functions like breathing and heart rate. This is serious but less frequent compared to transtentorial herniation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cingulate herniation (Option D): This is essentially another term for subfalcine herniation, where the cingulate gyrus moves under the falx cerebri, across the midline. It is typically less severe than a transtentorial herniation and may present with fewer noticeable symptoms."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 32 y/o man from a rural area presents with recurrent seizures. He recalls occasionally eating undercooked pork and living in a community with poor sanitation. A brain MRI shows multiple cystic lesions. What is the most likely cause of his condition?","o":[{"t":"Infection by adult Taenia solium tapeworms in the intestines"},{"t":"Ingestion of T. solium eggs from contaminated food or hands"},{"t":"Eating undercooked pork containing adult tapeworms"},{"t":"Migration of larvae from the lungs to the brain"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurocysticercosis occurs when humans ingest T. solium eggs , typically due to faecal contamination from an individual carrying the intestinal tapeworm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The eggs hatch into larvae in the intestines, which then enter the bloodstream and migrate to tissues like the brain, where they form cysts."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This process can lead to symptoms such as seizures, which are caused by the inflammation and irritation associated with cysts in the brain."},{"k":"img","v":8153},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infection by adult Taenia solium tapeworms in the intestines (Option A): Intestinal infection with adult T. solium tapeworms causes taeniasis, not neurocysticercosis. Taeniasis occurs when humans eat undercooked pork containing larval cysts (cysticerci), which develop into adult tapeworms in the intestines. However, this form of infection does not lead to the formation of cysts in the brain or cause seizures."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eating undercooked pork containing adult tapeworms (Option C): Eating undercooked pork exposes a person to larval cysts (cysticerci), not adult tapeworms. This leads to taeniasis, where the cysts mature into adult tapeworms in the intestines. This does not cause neurocysticercosis, as the infection of the brain occurs when eggs, not cysts, are ingested."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Migration of larvae from the lungs to the brain (Option D): The larvae of T. solium do not migrate from the lungs to the brain. After ingestion of eggs, the larvae penetrate the intestinal wall and travel through the bloodstream to reach various tissues, including the brain. The lungs are not involved in this migration process in neurocysticercosis."}]},{"n":15,"q":"Which of the following conditions is known to mimic coma but does not involve actual loss of consciousness?","o":[{"t":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"t":"Uremic encephalopathy"},{"t":"Hypoglycemia-induced coma"},{"t":"Locked-in syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Locked-in syndrome results from brainstem damage, specifically affecting motor pathways. LIS has been reported from either blunt or penetrating trauma leading to either a tear or thrombotic occlusion of the vertebrobasilar artery blood supply. Patients are completely conscious but unable to move, except for possible eye movements. This condition can be mistaken for coma because the patient appears unresponsive, but they retain full cognitive awareness, distinguishing it from true comatose states."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reversible Coma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Reversible Coma Treatment of Reversible Coma (h 188)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Metabolic Coma: Immediate Interventions: Results from lack of oxygen/glucose or disrupted Prevent further brain damage. neuronal excitability. Correct hypotension, hypoglycemia, Conditions include hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, hypoxia, hypercapnia. hypercalcemia, and hepatic/renal failure. (Option C ruled Airway Management: out) Oropharyngeal airway for drowsy patients Hepatic encephalopathy and uremic encephalopathy with normal breathing. cause coma due to elevated ammonia levels and Tracheal intubation for apnea, obstruction, elevated creatinine/ guanidine respectively. (Option A or aspiration risk. and B ruled out) Medications: Drug/Toxin-Induced Coma: Naloxone for opioid overdose. It is caused by drugs or toxins that affect the brainstem's Dextrose for hypoglycemia. reticular activating system (RAS) and cerebral cortex. Thiamine for malnourished patients Some drugs, like barbiturates, may mimic brain death (prevent Wernicke’s encephalopathy). signs. Physostigmine for anticholinergic overdose Epileptic Coma: (with caution). Generalised seizures (nonconvulsive status epilepticus) Fomepizole for ethylene glycol poisoning. can cause coma. Additional Considerations: EEG is essential for detecting treatable seizures in ... (content truncated) unexplained coma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hepatic encephalopathy (Option A), Uremic encephalopathy (Option B), Hypoglycemia-induced coma (Option C): All three conditions are metabolic encephalopathies that induce true coma."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 50 y/o woman presents with persistent headaches, double vision, and vomiting and also experiences worsening morning headaches. Examination shows reactive pupils and limited right eye lateral movement. Which cranial nerve is most likely affected by increased intracranial pressure?","o":[{"t":"Optic nerve (CN II)"},{"t":"Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"},{"t":"Trochlear nerve (CN IV)"},{"t":"Abducens nerve (CN VI)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The abducens nerve (CN VI) is commonly affected in cases of increased ICP, leading to lateral gaze difficulty (inability to move the eye outward) and diplopia (double vision), as seen in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Raised ICP - Clinical Features :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache : Resulting from irritation and stretching of the dura mater, which is innervated by the trigeminal nerve (CN V)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting : Often sudden, without nausea, and may be projectile in nature."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual Disturbances : These may include vision loss, flashing lights (photopsia), and double vision (diplopia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abducens Nerve Palsy : Cranial nerve 6 dysfunctions, leading to an inability to gaze laterally."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered Mental Status : Ranges from drowsiness to coma, depending on the severity of ICP elevation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unequal pupils due to brainstem compression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cushing's triad: Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Optic nerve (CN II) (Option A) : This nerve is responsible for vision, not eye movement. Although it can be affected by increased ICP (e.g., leading to papilledema), it does not cause gaze difficulties."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oculomotor nerve (CN III) (Option B) : This nerve controls most eye movements, including upward, downward, and medial gaze, as well as pupil constriction. While it can be affected by increased ICP, it typically presents with ptosis (drooping eyelid) and down and out pupil, not isolated lateral gaze difficulty."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oculomotor nerve palsy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Trochlear nerve (CN IV) (Option C) : This nerve primarily innervates the superior oblique muscle, which is responsible for downward and inward eye movement. Dysfunction may lead to vertical diplopia, but it does not typically cause difficulty with lateral gaze."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 35 y/o male is declared brain dead after a severe head injury. His family consents to organ donation. Which of the following organs is most commonly transplanted from a brain-dead donor?","o":[{"t":"Pancreas"},{"t":"Heart"},{"t":"Liver"},{"t":"Kidney"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Organ Transplantation from the Brain-Dead"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Organ donation occurs after confirming brain death. Two doctors with specific qualifications must confirm brain death through tests separately. Although brain dead, the patient remains ventilated to keep organs viable for transplantation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Organ Transplantation Indication Kidney Transplantation (Option D) Most common transplantation . Performed for end-stage"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"renal disease (ESRD). Common causes are Diabetic nephropathy, hypertension, and glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Liver Transplantation (Option C ruled out) Second most common transplantation. Done for"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cirrhosis, liver cancers, acute hepatic failure and cholestatic diseases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heart Transplantation (Option B ruled out) Refractory heart failure due to cardiomyopathy, coronary"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"artery disease, and congenital defects."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lung Transplantation Performed for severe respiratory failure, e.g., idiopathic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pulmonary fibrosis, emphysema, cystic fibrosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pancreas Transplantation (Option A ruled out) Often combined with kidney transplantation in diabetic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"patients with ESRD to reverse diabetes and its complications."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 38 y/o man with severe brain injury is undergoing tests to confirm brain death. The apnoea test is part of the evaluation. What is the purpose of the apnoea test in brain death diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"To assess spontaneous respiratory effort"},{"t":"To evaluate cardiac function during hypoxia"},{"t":"To measure oxygenation levels during ventilation"},{"t":"To monitor brainstem motor reflexes"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brain Death Investigations"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brain death refers to the permanent cessation of all brain and brainstem functions while cardiac activity and other bodily functions are maintained artificially. It is legally and ethically equivalent to death."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Preconditions for Brain Death Testing Deep coma with no influence of depressant drugs."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"No hypothermia (body temperature >35°C). Normal electrolyte, acid-base, and glucose levels, no metabolic or endocrine causes. No spontaneous respiration due to ventilatory dependence. The underlying condition causing brain damage must be irremediable."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Tests to Confirm Brain Death Absence of brainstem reflexes :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Fixed, non-reactive pupils. Absent corneal and vestibulo-ocular reflexes. No motor response to cranial nerve stimulation. Absent gag reflex and no response to tracheal suctioning. Apnoea Test : Checks for Spontaneous respiratory effort. (Option A) There is no respiratory effort in brain death when disconnected from the ventilator, despite PaCO2 rising above 50 mmHg."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Additional Investigations A four-vessel angiogram or isotope brain scan is used to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"confirm the absence of cerebral blood flow, particularly in complex cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Testing Protocol : Must be performed by two experienced doctors and repeated after a short interval to confirm the diagnosis. Once brain death is confirmed, organ donation may be considered with appropriate consent."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To evaluate cardiac function during hypoxia (Option B): The apnoea test is focused on respiratory function, not cardiac function."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To measure oxygenation levels during ventilation (Option C): Although oxygenation is also monitored during the test, the main goal is to assess respiratory drive, not oxygenation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To monitor brainstem motor reflexes (Option D): The apnoea test specifically evaluates respiratory function, not motor reflexes."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 45 y/o male presents to the ED after experiencing a severe headache, nausea, and confusion for the past few hours. O/E, the physician observes a slow heart rate, high blood pressure, and irregular respiratory patterns. What other clinical finding is most likely to be observed in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Decreased body temperature"},{"t":"Increased pupil size on the affected side"},{"t":"Photophobia"},{"t":"Elevated blood glucose levels"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased pupil size on the affected side (Hutchinson pupil) is a classic sign associated with increased intracranial pressure, especially in cases where there is pressure on the oculomotor nerve, leading to dilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cushing’s Reflex: A late sign of increased intracranial pressure characterized by: Hypertension : Elevated blood pressure. Bradycardia : Slow heart rate, typically fewer than 60 beats per minute. Irregular Breathing : Gasping for air (agonal breathing)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension : Elevated blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bradycardia : Slow heart rate, typically fewer than 60 beats per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregular Breathing : Gasping for air (agonal breathing)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Breathing Patterns: Cheyne-Stokes Breathing: Characterized by alternating periods of hyperventilation and apnea. It can occur in normal individuals at high altitudes and in those with severe neurological or cardiac conditions. Kussmaul respiration : Characterized by deep, rapid breathing, occurs in metabolic acidosis as the body attempts to expel carbon dioxide and lower blood acid levels. It can be caused by conditions like uremia or toxic ingestions, including alcohol and salicylates. Biot Respiration: Also periodic but lacks the crescendo-decrescendo pattern of Cheyne-Stokes. It consists of clusters of irregular breaths followed by periods of apnea, typically seen in individuals with pontine lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cheyne-Stokes Breathing: Characterized by alternating periods of hyperventilation and apnea. It can occur in normal individuals at high altitudes and in those with severe neurological or cardiac conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul respiration : Characterized by deep, rapid breathing, occurs in metabolic acidosis as the body attempts to expel carbon dioxide and lower blood acid levels. It can be caused by conditions like uremia or toxic ingestions, including alcohol and salicylates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biot Respiration: Also periodic but lacks the crescendo-decrescendo pattern of Cheyne-Stokes. It consists of clusters of irregular breaths followed by periods of apnea, typically seen in individuals with pontine lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hutchinson Pupil: Initial ipsilateral pupil contraction occurs due to intracranial pressure. As pressure increases, the ipsilateral pupil dilates and becomes non-reactive to light. Further pressure increase affects the contralateral pupil in a similar way (dilated, non-reactive). Fixed, dilated pupils indicate life-threatening tentorial herniation and brainstem compression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial ipsilateral pupil contraction occurs due to intracranial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As pressure increases, the ipsilateral pupil dilates and becomes non-reactive to light."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Further pressure increase affects the contralateral pupil in a similar way (dilated, non-reactive)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fixed, dilated pupils indicate life-threatening tentorial herniation and brainstem compression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension : Elevated blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bradycardia : Slow heart rate, typically fewer than 60 beats per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregular Breathing : Gasping for air (agonal breathing)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cheyne-Stokes Breathing: Characterized by alternating periods of hyperventilation and apnea. It can occur in normal individuals at high altitudes and in those with severe neurological or cardiac conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul respiration : Characterized by deep, rapid breathing, occurs in metabolic acidosis as the body attempts to expel carbon dioxide and lower blood acid levels. It can be caused by conditions like uremia or toxic ingestions, including alcohol and salicylates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biot Respiration: Also periodic but lacks the crescendo-decrescendo pattern of Cheyne-Stokes. It consists of clusters of irregular breaths followed by periods of apnea, typically seen in individuals with pontine lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial ipsilateral pupil contraction occurs due to intracranial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As pressure increases, the ipsilateral pupil dilates and becomes non-reactive to light."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Further pressure increase affects the contralateral pupil in a similar way (dilated, non-reactive)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fixed, dilated pupils indicate life-threatening tentorial herniation and brainstem compression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased body temperature (Option A) : This is not typically associated with increased intracranial pressure; patients often have a normal or elevated temperature depending on the underlying cause."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Photophobia (Option C) : While photophobia can occur in various neurological conditions, it is not a direct sign of increased intracranial pressure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Elevated blood glucose levels (Option D) : Elevated glucose levels are not a standard clinical finding associated with increased intracranial pressure."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 45 y/o male presents to the emergency department after a cardiac arrest. He was resuscitated and remains comatose. A CT scan reveals cerebral oedema and midline shift, indicating raised intracranial pressure (ICP). Which Tier 2 therapy would be most appropriate for managing his elevated ICP?","o":[{"t":"Therapeutic hypothermia"},{"t":"Decompressive hemicraniectomy"},{"t":"Continuous sedation"},{"t":"Osmotic therapy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tier 1 Therapy:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lowering ICP include analgesia, sedation, normocapnia, external ventricular drain (EVD) placement, and hyperosmolar therapy (mannitol 20% or hypertonic saline). These treatments may cause fluid and electrolyte imbalances, so ICP-guided administration is recommended. EVD placement can be challenging and carries risks like haemorrhage, misplacement, and infection. While effective, tier 1 therapies should be cautiously used, as higher-level interventions have been linked to worse outcomes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tier 2 Therapy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tier 2 therapy refers to advanced interventions for managing persistent elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) when initial treatments (Tier 1) are ineffective. Both therapeutic hypothermia and decompressive hemicraniectomy aim to minimise brain damage and enhance patient outcomes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Therapeutic Hypothermia (32-36°C) Indication : Primarily for hypoxic-ischemic (Option A) encephalopathy (HIE) following cardiac arrest."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mechanism : Slows neuronal injury processes by lowering body temperature, potentially reducing brain damage. Clinical Evidence : Proven effective in improving outcomes for comatose patients after resuscitation, with trials showing benefits at mild hypothermia (33°C). Guidelines : Recommended for 24 hours in unresponsive cardiac arrest patients, target temperature is typically 32-36°C."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decompressive Hemicraniectomy Indication : For refractory elevated ICP that persists (Option B ruled out) despite other treatments."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Procedure : Involves surgically removing a portion of the skull to relieve pressure on the swollen brain. Clinical Evidence : Shown to improve outcomes in select patients, especially in cases of severe traumatic brain injury and significant brain swelling."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pentobarbital Coma Indication : Considered for refractory ICP"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Application : It is not as commonly used as other therapies but may be applied in specific circumstances."},{"k":"img","v":8165},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decompressive craniectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continuous sedation (Option C): This can reduce agitation and metabolic demand but is not a Tier 2 therapy for managing refractory ICP. It's more of a supportive measure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Osmotic therapy (Option D): While helpful in lowering ICP, it is considered a Tier 1 intervention. It may be employed before progressing to Tier 2 therapies."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A patient in the ICU exhibits signs of severe brain dysfunction and demonstrates decerebrate posturing. What should the healthcare team consider regarding intracranial pressure (ICP) management?","o":[{"t":"ICP monitoring is unnecessary and they should focus on other interventions"},{"t":"Immediate intervention is required for elevated ICP"},{"t":"The prognosis is good despite posturing"},{"t":"Avoid ventriculostomy as it may worsen the condition"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The occurrence of decerebrate posturing signifies severe neurological impairment, necessitating prompt assessment and management of intracranial pressure to prevent secondary brain injury and optimize patient outcomes. It is crucial to monitor ICP closely to determine the need for potential interventions."},{"k":"img","v":8168},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Posturing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The decorticate posturing indicates b/l damage to the rostral part of the midbrain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The decerebrate posturing indicates damage to the motor tracts caudal to the midbrain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ICP monitoring is unnecessary and they should focus on other interventions (Option A): Decerebrate posturing is a serious indicator of potential increased intracranial pressure. Ignoring the need for ICP monitoring can lead to critical delays in addressing possible elevations in pressure, which could further worsen the patient's neurological status."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The prognosis is good despite posturing (Option C): The presence of posturing, particularly decerebrate posturing, typically reflects significant brain injury and is often associated with a poor prognosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Avoid ventriculostomy as it may worsen the condition (Option D): Ventriculostomy is a vital procedure for monitoring and managing intracranial pressure, especially in patients showing signs of severe brain injury and posturing. Failing to perform a ventriculostomy could prevent timely intervention for elevated ICP, which is crucial for the patient's care."},{"k":"img","v":8173},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventriculostomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventriculostomy is a surgical procedure that involves inserting a catheter into one of the brain's lateral ventricles."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Benefits of ventriculostomy :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Real-Time Monitoring : Facilitates immediate assessment of ICP, guiding treatment decisions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pressure Relief : Reduces ICP through CSF drainage, potentially improving patient outcomes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Guides Treatment : Informs other therapies, such as osmotic therapy or sedation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Preference : Generally favoured over parenchymal ICP monitoring when CSF drainage is necessary."}]},{"n":22,"q":"A patient with fulminant hepatic failure is admitted to the intensive care unit (ICU). The patient is at risk of increased intracranial pressure due to cerebral oedema. What is the primary goal of management ICP in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Keep ICP below 20 mmHg"},{"t":"Keep ICP below 40 mmHg"},{"t":"Keep CPP below 20 mmHg"},{"t":"Liver transplantation without ICP monitoring"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ICP Monitoring"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Critical for managing patients with severe brain injury. Real-time ICP measurements enable timely interventions and treatment adjustments to prevent further brain injury."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indications for ICP Monitoring : Severe Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI): Especially when"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score is 8 or lower. Major Brain Tissue Displacement Large Ischemic or Hemorrhagic Strokes Hydrocephalus Fulminant Hepatic Failure"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Methods of ICP Monitoring Ventriculostomy : Involves inserting a catheter into the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"brain’s lateral ventricles. Allows for direct measurement of ICP and therapeutic drainage of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to lower pressure. Parenchymal Monitoring : Involves placing a sensor directly into brain tissue. Used when ventriculostomy is not suitable due to diffuse oedema or coagulopathy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Goals of ICP Management Maintain ICP below 20 mmHg : To prevent brain"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ischemia and herniation . (Option A) (Option B ruled out) Maintain Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) above 60 mmHg : Ensures adequate blood flow to the brain despite elevated ICP. (Option C ruled out) Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is the pressure gradient that drives blood flow to the brain. Adequate CPP is essential for delivering oxygen and nutrients to neuronal tissues while removing metabolic waste products. It is calculated using the formula : CPP = Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) - Intracranial Pressure (ICP)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Liver transplantation without ICP monitoring (Option D): Although liver transplantation is the definitive intervention for fulminant hepatic failure, neglecting ICP monitoring could result in secondary brain injury related to cerebral oedema."}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 35 y/o male is brought to the ED after a road traffic accident. He is unconscious with a GCS score of 6. His blood pressure is 160/100 mmHg, and he exhibits symptoms of bradycardia and irregular respiration. What is the first step in managing his raised intracranial pressure?","o":[{"t":"Perform hyperventilation"},{"t":"Administer mannitol"},{"t":"Elevate head end"},{"t":"Administer 0.45% NaCl"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Raised Intracranial Pressure (ICP)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes Trauma, tumours, haemorrhage, or hydrocephalus can"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lead to increased ICP."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms Headache, vomiting, altered consciousness,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"papilledema (swelling of the optic disc), unequal pupils due to brainstem compression Cushing's triad: Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Raised ICP (when ICP>20-25mmHg for >5min)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Initial Measures Head elevation : Raising the head to 30 degrees (Option C) enhances venous drainage and reduces ICP by"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"promoting blood flow out of the brain. Sedation : Sedating the patient with agents like midazolam reduces metabolic demands on the brain, helping control ICP."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medical Therapies Osmotherapy (Mannitol or Hypertonic saline) : (Option B"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out) Mannitol acts as an osmotic diuretic, reducing cerebral oedema by drawing fluid from brain tissue. Hypertonic saline works similarly by pulling water from brain cells into the bloodstream, lowering brain swelling. Contraindication: Active intracranial bleeding Glucocorticoids : Effective in reducing vasogenic oedema, particularly in cases involving tumours, but not used for trauma-induced ICP."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ventilation Strategies Hyperventilation: Temporarily reduces ICP by inducing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cerebral vasoconstriction, leading to decreased blood flow. This is a short-term measure that requires careful monitoring to avoid ischemia. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CSF Drainage In cases of hydrocephalus or ventricular enlargement,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) can be drained through a catheter to relieve pressure. Ventriculostomy is the procedure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pressor therapy Common pressors like phenylephrine, dopamine, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"norepinephrine raise MAP to ensure cerebral blood flow and relieve ischemia by promoting autoregulatory vasoconstriction and reducing intracranial blood volume. Pressor therapy is used only after other treatments fail."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Advanced Therapies Decompressive craniectomy : A surgical procedure"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"where part of the skull is removed to allow the brain to swell without causing further pressure buildup. This is considered when ICP remains uncontrolled by other measures."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer 0.45% NaCl (Option D): Hypotonic solutions are avoided in patients with raised ICP. It can lead to a further increase in ICP."}]}]);
